Pressure Injuries (Ulcers)
Geriatric & Frailty
Non-blanchable erythema over bony prominence, Full-thickness tissue loss, Necrotic eschar/slough, History of prolonged immobility

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-039
Reason for Visit: Acute severe pain, foul odour, and sudden worsening of a chronic wound on the lower back in a bedbound patient.
Location: Aged Care Facility
Present at Visit: Patient and nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an immobile patient with a rapidly deteriorating, malodorous sacral wound, causing severe new-onset pain. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to evaluate for acute secondary bacterial infection, underlying osteomyelitis, or systemic sepsis, providing immediate wound care and antibiotic therapy prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My lower back is burning constantly, and the nurses say the sore has gotten much deeper since yesterday."
History of Present Illness: The nursing staff report a history of prolonged immobility; the patient is confined to a bed and chair due to advanced frailty. They describe the initial presentation two weeks ago as a localized area of non-blanchable erythema situated directly over the sacrum (bony prominence). Over the past week, this progressed to partial-thickness loss of dermis. Today, the staff called due to a sudden deterioration: the wound now exhibits full-thickness skin and tissue loss with visible subcutaneous fat. There is necrotic eschar (black tissue) and yellow slough firmly adhered to the ulcer base. Most concerningly, the staff note a foul-smelling purulent exudate indicating a secondary bacterial infection. The patient reports severe, burning pain at the site, which is a change from their baseline. The surrounding skin demonstrates intense erythema, induration, and warmth. They deny high fevers or rigors.
Functional Status: Completely bedbound and fully dependent for all repositioning and pressure care.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of systemic sepsis and deep bone infection (osteomyelitis).

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 95 | Temp: 37.6 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 55 Kg
General: Frail, lying on their side, grimacing, room has a distinct malodorous scent.
Physical Exam: Integumentary exam of the sacral region reveals a large (approx. 6x6 cm) Stage 3 pressure injury. The wound bed contains 40% necrotic black eschar and 60% yellow slough. There is active, foul-smelling, thick purulent exudate. The peri-wound skin shows intense erythema extending 3 cm from the wound edge, with marked induration and warmth consistent with acute localized cellulitis. Gentle probing (with a sterile swab) does not currently contact deep bone (Stage 4), but the wound is deep. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams are unremarkable. Neurological exam confirms the patient has intact sensation in the sacral area, explaining the severe pain. (Clinical diagnosis based on standardized staging with deep tissue culture demonstrating pathogenic growth is an anticipated diagnostic tetrad finding).
Environmental Observations: The patient is lying on a standard foam mattress, which is vastly insufficient for their level of risk and immobility.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking regular paracetamol, which is insufficient for the acute nociceptive and inflammatory pain of the infected ulcer.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Infected Stage 3 Sacral Pressure Injury with localized peri-wound cellulitis - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Necrotizing Fasciitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The erythema is localized and not rapidly spreading (within hours), there is no crepitus (gas in the tissues) on palpation, and the patient does not exhibit profound systemic toxicity or shock (normal BP).

Pure Neuropathic Ulcer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The location on the sacrum (pressure-bearing) rather than the plantar surface of the foot, the patient's intact sensation (pain), and the explicit history of prolonged immobility rather than ambulating on a numb foot.

Acute Osteomyelitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Cannot be definitively ruled out clinically tonight without deep tissue biopsy/imaging, but the immediate priority is treating the acute superficial cellulitis and purulence; probe-to-bone test is currently negative.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Risk of sepsis and massive tissue loss)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a rapidly deteriorating, infected sacral pressure ulcer complicated by acute localized cellulitis and severe pain. Telephone triage correctly identified the high risk of impending systemic sepsis in a frail adult. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have resulted in unmanaged severe pain, unchecked local tissue destruction, and highly likely progression to systemic bacteremia and septic shock. This urgent after-hours intervention allowed for immediate physical staging of the wound, the initiation of targeted systemic antibiotic therapy to halt the cellulitis, the optimization of analgesia, and the immediate enforcement of strict pressure-relieving strategies by the facility staff.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Commenced a 7-day course of oral broad-spectrum antibiotics covering skin flora and anaerobes (e.g., amoxicillin/clavulanate 875/125mg). Prescribed a short course of oral opiate analgesia (e.g., oxycodone 2.5mg - 5mg) to be given strictly 30 minutes prior to all wound dressings and repositioning. Cleansed the wound with sterile saline and applied an antimicrobial absorbent dressing (e.g., silver-impregnated foam). Ordered a formal wound swab for culture and sensitivities.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a strict "turn and chart" routine for the nursing staff. Step 1: Staff must physically reposition the patient every two hours (left side, right side, back) and immediately sign a highly visible repositioning chart at the foot of the bed. Step 2: Upon successful completion of a 12-hour shift with 100% compliance on the chart, the charge nurse provides specific, recorded positive feedback to the care team. Cause-and-effect: Mandating a highly visible physical chart directly enforces the critical mechanical offloading required to allow the wound to perfuse. The positive reinforcement ensures staff compliance, directly accelerating wound healing and preventing new ulcer formation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Initiated an urgent referral to the facility's specialized wound care nurse for formal debridement of the necrotic eschar.
DME/Safety (Optional): Urgently mandated the immediate deployment of an alternating pressure air mattress.
Follow-up: Routine GP review required within 48-72 hours to review the wound culture results and assess the response to antibiotics.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department if the patient develops a high fever, uncontrollable shaking (rigors), acute confusion, or if the red, hot area around the wound spreads rapidly down the buttocks or back.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The wound has developed a severe bacterial infection that requires immediate antibiotic treatment and strict pressure relief; deep bone infection remains a possibility requiring further testing.
Clinical Course: The antibiotics will stop the spreading redness and reduce the foul odor over the next 3 days. The black, dead tissue will require specialized removal by a wound nurse before true healing can begin.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

(Directed to nursing staff): Transfer the patient to the Emergency Department immediately if they spike a fever, become acutely confused, or if the redness spreads rapidly. A doctor must review the wound swab results later this week.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because you cannot move yourself, the constant pressure of the bed cuts off the blood supply to your skin, causing the tissue to die. That dead tissue has now become infected with bacteria, causing the pain and the smell. We have started strong antibiotics to kill the bacteria, and stronger painkillers to keep you comfortable.
Actionable Steps: You must allow the nurses to turn you every two hours, even if it is uncomfortable. You must be moved onto a specialized air mattress immediately to remove the pressure from your lower back.

